<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client C#</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-dotnet</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Thin Maven wrapper over the C# toolchain. This module is in the reactor only under
        -Dpc.foreignClients - see the foreign-clients profile in the clients aggregator for why
        opt-in is load-bearing - and all it does today is build a program that prints one fixture
        line and check that it did (astubbs#242).

        The .csproj beside this pom is the language-native build file, and Directory.Build.props
        redirects both the intermediate and the output directories under target/ - MSBuild's
        defaults are obj/ and bin/ in the module root, which would leave output behind that
        Maven's default clean never sees.

        THE CONFIGURATION IS NAMED ON BOTH SIDES. MSBuild appends it to BaseOutputPath, so the
        assembly lands in target/bin/Debug/ rather than target/bin/ - and a run command that
        guessed would break the day somebody built Release. Spelling it in the build command and
        in the path is one fact written twice in a way that cannot silently disagree: change one
        and the run fails immediately.

        Both commands go through bin/foreign-client-step.sh, which decides what an ABSENT dotnet
        means: a banner and a skip by default, red under -Dpc.foreign.strict=1, which is what the
        CI row passes. The commands themselves are declared here and nowhere else.
    -->
    <properties>
        <pc.foreign.skip>false</pc.foreign.skip>
        <pc.foreign.build.executable>bash</pc.foreign.build.executable>
        <pc.foreign.build.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool dotnet -- dotnet build --nologo --verbosity quiet --configuration Debug</pc.foreign.build.args>
        <pc.foreign.test.executable>bash</pc.foreign.test.executable>
        <pc.foreign.test.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool dotnet --hello dotnet -- dotnet target/bin/Debug/hello.dll</pc.foreign.test.args>
    </properties>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <!--
                NO maven-clean-plugin FILESET HERE, and that is a decision rather than an omission -
                "nothing to clean" and "nobody wired the clean" look identical in a pom. Everything
                this module builds lands under target/, which Maven's default clean already takes.
                Directory.Build.props points BaseIntermediateOutputPath and BaseOutputPath under target/, precisely so the default clean is enough.
            -->
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
